  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front remains stalled to our southwest, with high \npressure off to the northeast of Maine. The high gradually moves\noff to the east tonight, while the warm front lifts north. A \nwave of low pressure moves along this front tonight, bringing a \nwidespread round or rain, heavy showers, and some thunderstorms.\n\nThe front edge of the showers arrives into northern New \nHampshire around sunset, and then spreads south and east through\nthe evening hours. A few thunderstorms will be possible as well\nthrough the evening across western New Hampshire. Severe storms\nare expected across Upstate New York today, but by the time \nthey reach New Hampshire the convection is expected to be \nelevated and not pose a severe threat.\n\nThe showers and storms evolve into a steady period of rain \novernight with embedded heavier downpours. Rain is expected \nthrough the overnight, with the back edge moving from west to \neast during the morning hours on Thursday. Breaks of sun are \nlikely across New Hampshire during the day tomorrow, bringing \nthe chance for scattered pop up showers during the afternoon \nhours. Overall rainfall amounts generally look to fall in the \n0.75 to 1.25 in range.\n\nThe warm front is expected to stall somewhere in western or \ncentral Maine. East of the front, low clouds and sprinkles \nremain through the afternoon hours. It won't be until an \noccluded front passes through tomorrow night that drier air \nmoves back into the region. Clearing is expected behind this \nfront Thursday night, with nighttime valley fog likely by late \nin the night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper low will slowly dumbbell down from central Quebec through \nnorthern Maine and over to Nova Scotia Friday into Saturday. While \nFriday will mainly feature dry conditions, a few showers will be \npossible in the afternoon over and north of the mountains. A better \nchance for more widespread showers may evolve Saturday afternoon as \na vort lobe rotates down around the upper low to our east. Model \nsoundings suggest that there will be at least some modest \ninstability in place so a few rumbles of thunder may be possible, \nespecially over southern New Hampshire where instability will likely \nbe maximized. Persistent northwest flow will lead to cooler \ntemperatures and dewpoints through the holiday weekend with highs \nmainly in the mid 60s north to the mid 70s south and some breezier \nwinds out of the north. An approaching ridge may lead to a slight \nwarmup on Labor Day with some low precipitation chances returning to \nthe forecast Monday night into Tuesday.",
